db/id #edb/ref 17592186105744 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46cd136a-cc20-51e1-ac90-f8d9b787c5b3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Powers of Negative Fractions Using the Power of Quotient Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "80cde83c-b7fb-5ad7-bc3e-528e47151da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4673" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\dfrac{3}{2}=\\dfrac{(-3)}{2}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^3=\\left(\\dfrac{(-3)}{2}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}\n$$\n\nNow, computing $(-3)^3=-27$ and $2^3=8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}= \\dfrac{-27}{8}=-\\dfrac{27}{8} .\n$$"}]}
